WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
"Can Cold Showers Improve Immune Function?"

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.640
Several studies
have demonstrated

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.600
that cold temperatures
may be a potential risk factor

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.440
for cancer development.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.440
For example, this study
compared the 50 countries

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.120
with the highest cancer rates

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.520
to the countries
with the lowest cancer rates

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.480
and concluded that people
living in cold environments

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.480
may have a higher risk
of getting

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.720
and dying from cancer.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.480
A similar study
within the United States

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.640
found that colder counties
were associated

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.720
with a greater cancer incidence.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.440
But couldn't something
like vitamin D exposure

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.360
better explain these findings?

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.760
Patients diagnosed with cancer
during summer months

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.280
may have a better prognosis

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.360
than those diagnosed
during the winter.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.320
But again, this could be

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.800
because of the sunshine vitamin,
vitamin D.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.120
Evidently, an animal colony
had less cancer

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.800
when they moved
from a colder country

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.160
to a warmer country

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.760
based on some observation
published in 1939.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.680
Seems a bit of a stretch,

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.360
but then this study
was published.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.560
Standard housing temperature

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.800
for laboratory mice
in research facilities

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.680
is mandated to be between
20 and 26 Celsius,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.360
like typical room temperature
in the 70s Fahrenheit.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.360
But, that's chilly for mice,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.160
causing chronic
mild cold stress.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.680
When that stress
is alleviated

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.120
by housing
at warmer temperatures,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.880
like in the 80s Fahrenheit
(27 - 32 °C),

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.360
researchers observed a striking
reduction in tumor formation,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.360
cancer growth rate,
and metastasis.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.520
Warmer was better.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.360
They suggested
that the cold stress

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.440
results in a suppression
of antitumor immunity.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.960
Then, why do they keep mice
at that lower temperature?

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.600
It reduces the frequency
of cage cleaning

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.360
and helps ensure the thermal
comfort of the staff.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.080
The proposed mechanism
is the activation

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.720
of the fight-or-flight response
that leads to the release

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.960
of the stress hormone
norepinephrine,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.360
also known as noradrenalin.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.120
Take a dip
in a typical cold plunge,

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.280
and there can be a doubling
of norepinephrine levels

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.720
in the blood within minutes.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.400
And we don't seem to habituate,

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.880
meaning we get that same bump
in norepinephrine

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.320
after each cold-water immersion,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.200
even after 3 months
of regular plunges.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.360
Might that be suppressing
immune function?

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.920
Or might it instead be hormesis,

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.720
that which doesn't kill us
makes us stronger?

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.920
Cold-water swimmers
claim to suffer fewer

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.800
and milder infections
as a result of the practice.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.760
Even if that's true,
cold-water swimmers

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.200
are swimming.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.520
Maybe it's the exercise,
not the cold?

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.640
How would we figure this out?

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.800
To control for any effect
of swimming,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.360
researchers compared
cold-water swimmers

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.560
to indoor heated-pool swimmers
and found no difference

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.360
in upper respiratory tract
susceptibility between swimmers

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.200
in cold water and heated pools.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.640
Therefore, cold-water swimming

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.760
appears to have
no protective effect.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.960
What about regularly
taking cold showers?

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.680
Cold showers were first designed
for the purpose

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.000
of "treating the insane,"

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.240
and then used as an instrument
of torture in prisons

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.200
before it started
killing too many people.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.880
Rather than being abandoned,
however,

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.280
the shower was transformed
by the use of warm water

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.480
to actually clean people.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.080
I had no idea.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.040
If you randomize people
to short daily cold showers

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.360
versus hot showers
for a few months,

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.800
regular cold shower exposure

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.920
does appear to put
the immune system on high alert.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.640
Does that actually translate
into less sickness?

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.640
There's only been this one
randomized trial

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.120
on The Effect of Cold Showering
on Health and Work:

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.960
A Randomized Controlled Trial.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.680
Participants were randomized
to shower as normal,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:40.960
but end their showers

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.360
with 0, 30, 60, or 90 seconds
of cold water.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.760
And compared
to the no cold group,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.280
the different cold-water groups
collectively had a 29% reduction

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.520
of self-reported sick leave
from work,

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.880
but no fewer self-reported days
of sickness.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.160
So, maybe the cold showering
made them less sick

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.040
even though they were sick
for the same duration?

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:03.920
The problem, of course,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.160
is that while
there was a control group,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:07.600
there was no placebo control.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.520
So, maybe
the cold showering groups

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.480
chose to skip work less
for some reason.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.400
It's not clear, but at least
it didn't seem to show

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.680
cold showering was harmful
to immune function

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.646
at least in the short-run
in healthy adults.

